                <blockquote>
<p><strong>Weekly editorial note:</strong> The key issue this week is not a single policy announcement. It is the shift from one-time document submission to continuous verification. Account identity, payment authorization, creative claims, and landing-page information must remain consistent; otherwise, both delivery and funding may be disrupted.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>1. What Changed: Compliance Risks Are Moving Across the Workflow</h2>
<p>A tool app replaced the payment entity for its ad accounts but continued using the previous company name on payment documents. The problem was not the payment itself, but the absence of consistency checks across the registered entity, invoice details, and funding source.</p>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team also used the phrase “lowest price online” in its creative while the landing page displayed prices for selected products only. The claim passed initial review, but subsequent page updates introduced new exposure. Creative review should therefore include forms, landing pages, promotional terms, and refund conditions.</p>
<p>For media buyers, account qualification is no longer a one-off filing exercise, and approved creative should not be assumed to remain compliant indefinitely. Compliance needs to become a scheduled, documented, and accountable operation.</p>
<h2>2. Recommended SOP: Seven Checks Every 30 Minutes Each Week</h2>
<h3>1. Verify Entity and Authorization</h3>
<p>Check whether the account name, business registration, website domain, and company information on the landing page match. For agency-managed accounts, confirm that authorization scope, agency relationships, and operator permissions are still valid. Update platform records whenever the registered entity changes.</p>
<h3>2. Review Top-ups and Payment Evidence</h3>
<p>Record the platform, currency, amount, payer, transaction number, and settlement status for every top-up. Confirm that the payment account belongs to an authorized entity and that agency-funded accounts have written customer approval. For duplicate charges, delayed credit, or unclear refunds, retain the transaction records and contact platform support instead of repeatedly submitting payments.</p>
<h3>3. Audit Multi-Account Access</h3>
<p>Maintain a register showing the platform, region, currency, purpose, legal entity, administrator, buyer, payer, and operational owner of each account. Prioritize the removal of former employees, temporary agency accounts, and shared credentials. Enable two-factor authentication and rotate sensitive permissions regularly.</p>
<h3>4. Review Creative Claims and Evidence</h3>
<p>Recheck absolute or authority-based statements such as “No. 1,” “Guaranteed,” “Permanent,” “Zero Risk,” or “Official Partner,” and retain evidence where the claim can be verified. Claims involving comparative language, discounts, health outcomes, performance, or earnings should be supported. If proof is unavailable, revise the claim rather than relying on review system tolerance.</p>
<h3>5. Match the Landing Page</h3>
<p>Ensure that the company name, contact details, prices, promotional conditions, refund policy, and ad promises are consistent. Update ads promptly after page changes, campaign expiration, or inventory updates to prevent broken links, unclear terms, and outdated offers.</p>
<h3>6. Flag Reconciliation Exceptions</h3>
<p>Match platform invoices, account spend, top-up orders, and accounting records line by line. Place unmatched items in a pending-review list with an owner and deadline rather than treating them as normal spend. Before classifying a variance as unauthorized billing, check currency, tax, time zones, exchange rates, and refunds.</p>
<h3>7. Escalate and Document</h3>
<p>Use three response levels: correct inconsistent records; pause affected ads when creative or landing pages are suspected of violating policy; and suspend top-ups while escalating when payment, authorization, or account authenticity is in doubt. Keep screenshots, links, version numbers, reviewers, conclusions, and action dates for appeals and post-mortem reviews.</p>
<h2>3. Recommendation: Put the Review on the Weekly Meeting Agenda</h2>
<p>Assign one compliance owner. At the start of each week, export account status, top-up records, recently changed creative, and anomalous invoices. Allow 30 minutes for review. If the same issue appears twice in an ad team, the media buyer, creative owner, landing-page owner, and finance team should conduct a joint review.</p>
<p>Most importantly, a lack of enforcement by a platform system is not proof of compliance. Platform review is only one part of risk management. Businesses should retain genuine supporting evidence and a complete audit trail for operational actions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Editorial note:</strong> Requirements vary by platform, region, account type, and industry. Teams should confirm current platform policies, contractual terms, and applicable local laws before adopting this SOP.</p>
</blockquote>
